kings/delegate/out/K10/REPORT.md:5:# K10: a certificate for passers (Remark 2.11), and the status of P11
kings/delegate/out/K10/REPORT.md:13:5. **P11 is now PROVED in P (revised after K10, 2026-10-22).** Theorem 1.11 + Theorem 1.14 (frozen kings give (V1) and (V3) by fiat) + the frozen passer certificate (Proposition 2.11) for (V2) on passer terminals, plus HSS for the o(S) part, complete the backing. (A_Q) is not involved (P-level).
kings/delegate/out/K10/REPORT.md:38:**Step 1 — No σ̄ pawn can block or capture p.** In cylcgt, `passed` for a White pawn on (f, r) requires that no Black pawn stands on files f − 1, f, f + 1 (mod W on a cylinder) with rank > r, and that the pawn is not e.p.-capturable. Concretely, on a cylinder of width W the three files are (f − 1) mod W, f, (f + 1) mod W, and "rank > r" means strictly ahead on White's side (for Black, rank < r). Thus no Black pawn can stand one file left, on the same file, or one file right, anywhere from r + 1 to H. Such a pawn would be needed to (a) block p's straight advance (same file, rank > r), (b) capture p (adjacent file, rank r + 1), or (c) be captured en passant (adjacent file, same rank, just double-stepped — excluded by the e.p. clause). The wrap is handled by the modular file arithmetic, so the seam (file a / last file) gives the same three-file neighbourhood. As p advances (r increases), the set of enemy pawns that could satisfy "rank > r" only shrinks, so a pawn that is passed stays passed under straight pushes. Hence no σ̄ pawn can ever block or capture p during the push.
kings/delegate/out/K10/REPORT.md:40:**Step 2 — σ's strategy "push p every move" is legal and wins the count.** p is the front pawn of its file, so every square ahead on that file is empty; straight pushes (single, or the double step from the second rank) are legal, and no own pawn blocks. By Step 1 no enemy pawn blocks or captures, and by hypothesis no enemy king stands on the path. So σ can push p on every one of its turns.
kings/delegate/out/K10/REPORT.md:49:**Step 3 — En passant cannot rescue σ̄.** Under the e.p.-aware test, a pawn that has just double-stepped and can be taken en passant is not passed (`PASSED_RESPECTS_EP = True`). The certificate's hypothesis requires p to be passed at the node, so at the creation node p is not e.p.-capturable: there is no σ̄ pawn on an adjacent file at the capture rank. The only way e.p. could rescue σ̄ is an adjacent pawn that captures p during the push, but Step 1 already rules out any adjacent enemy pawn ahead, and a pawn can only become an e.p. capturer by double-stepping into the adjacent file, which would have to be a pawn move by σ̄ — but σ̄'s pawns that could do this are on adjacent files, and if such a pawn double-steps it lands on a square that p's push passes through; p is not blocked because the capturer lands behind p's path. More directly: if p ever double-steps during the push, it becomes momentarily e.p.-capturable, but there is no adjacent σ̄ pawn at the capture rank (none exists by Step 1, and σ̄ cannot create one without a move, which would be spent and would still leave no capturer at the right rank in time). So e.p. gives σ̄ no win.
kings/delegate/out/K10/REPORT.md:51:**Step 4 — σ's own pawns cannot get in p's way.** p is the front pawn of its file, so no own pawn stands ahead on that file. Pawns on other files do not affect p's straight advance or its promotion square. p never needs to capture. If p double-steps, the intermediate square is empty by the front-pawn condition. Hence σ's other pawns never obstruct the push.
kings/delegate/out/K10/REPORT.md:89:**Coincidence with the count rule.** cylcgt's `RACE_RULE = "count"` scores a race by: the opponent of the mover moves first; whoever needs fewer moves to promote wins; the opponent wins ties. This is precisely the parity of Step 2: when σ creates a race (σ̄ to move), σ̄ moves first and wins ties (μ ≤ d), and σ wins only if strictly faster (μ ≥ d + 1). So the certificate's bound μ ≥ d + 1 is the count rule's "σ wins the race" condition, expressed in promotion distances. `promote_distance` (cylcgt) is the d of the certificate: it counts the double step and requires the front-pawn condition (no own pawn ahead), exactly as the certificate's d.
