kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:31:*Step 0 — three facts about F.* (F1) A pawn changes file only by a capture, landing on the square of the captured piece (an e.p. capture lands on the file of the captured pawn). There are no officers before the first promotion and a king is never captured, so a pawn enters a file only by capturing a pawn that stands on it, and a file without pawns never gets one. (F2) A king move ends F at once, so in any play in which its maker has not lost, no king moves and both kings stand where they stand at y. (F3) A promotion ends F with a win for the promoter.
kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:33:*Step 1 — safety of the kings.* By (H2) and (F1), no pawn ever stands on a file adjacent to β's king, so no ω pawn ever attacks it; ω's king gives no check. Thus β is never in check and no push of p is ruled out by king safety. By (H3) and (F1), the three files around t never hold a pawn, so t is never occupied, never attacked by a β pawn (p included, by the same file condition), and is not adjacent to β's king: a move of ω's king to t is always legal, even if ω is in check. So ω always has a legal move — it is never stalemated, and F has no draw by stalemate in the play below.
kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:35:*Step 2 — the invariant.* In plays where β follows the strategy Π (push p) and ω moves arbitrarily, let ρ_p be the current value of ρ(p) and call files f−1, f, f+1 the strip. We show:
kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:42:β moves. p goes from ρ_p to ρ′ ∈ {ρ_p+1, ρ_p+2}; the squares are empty by (I1), and the move is legal by Step 1. By (Jβ), the ω pawns in the strip have ρ ≤ ρ_p+1 ≤ ρ′. A double step (ρ′ = ρ_p+2) leaves every ω pawn strictly below ρ′, and an e.p. capturer would have to stand at ρ′ (the rank of p after the push), which (Jβ) forbids; a single step gives no e.p. right. So (Jω) holds, and (I1) still holds.
kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:46:*Step 3 — consequences.* At every ω turn no ω pawn stands at ρ_p+1 next to p and p cannot be taken e.p., so p is never captured; nothing stands ahead of p on file f ((I1)), so p is never blocked. At every β turn the push is legal. So Π can be played to the end.
kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:48:*Step 4 — ω's moves are moves of G_ω.* In a play consistent with Π, each ω pawn move is a move of G_ω(y;p): the squares and the captured pawns are the same, p never takes part (Step 3), β's other pawns stay (Π), the kings stay (F2), and the only e.p. capture is the one offered at y. G_ω has fewer obstacles, since p is deleted, which only enlarges its move set. So if ω promotes after k of its moves in the real play, then k ≥ μ_ω(y;p). An ω king move loses at once (F2).
kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:50:*Step 5 — timing.* Under Π, β promotes with its d-th push. Before that push ω has made exactly e moves (e = d if s = ω, e = d−1 if s = β). By (H5), e < μ_ω(y;p), so by Step 4 none of those moves promotes; by Step 1 ω has a legal move each time, so the play is not a draw. ω's only other moves are king moves, which lose at once. β's d-th push is legal (Step 3) and promotes, and by (F3) β wins. ∎
kings/delegate/out/K11/REPORT.md:58:| Step 0 (F1–F3) | SOUND | Standard facts of F; F1 is the empty-file invariance used everywhere. |
