kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:11:        "summary": "I read the proof of Proposition 2.11 and found no error in it. That covers Steps 0-5, the invariants (J_beta), (J_omega) and (I1), the timing, d = H - rho - [rho = 2] and the G_omega argument. Corollaries 2.11a and 2.11b also hold. The gaps are in how the P11 rows (status table and section 6) state their scope, and in a few places where the text is imprecise.\n\nPROOF CHECKS (by hand, against cylcgt/pawns.py and kings/kpsolve.c):\n- F1/F2/F3 match kpsolve --frozen. A king move, capture or not, emits a terminal win for the opponent. A promotion emits a terminal win for the promoter and is legal only if the promoter's king is not in check. A side with no move draws, or loses if in check, and king moves count as moves (kpsolve.c, `m == 0`).\n- Invariants under every kind of move:\n  - beta single push and double step: J_omega holds with (J_beta). No e.p. right can arise, because an e.p. capturer must stand on the destination rank rho_p+2 and J_beta caps omega pawns at rho_p+1.\n  - omega push and double step stay on their file and lower rho. Captures landing on file f are behind p by (H1). Captures landing on f+-1 land at rho(q) <= rho_0+1 by (H4), including captures from outside the strip. The e.p. capture offered at y lands at most one rank below its victim, and the victim is not p by (H1).\n  - Captures out of the strip change nothing. omega king moves end F (F2).\n- Step 4: every real omega move is a G_omega move. Deleting p and ignoring checks only enlarge the move set, and p is never captured.\n- Step 5: both orders give e = d (s = omega) and e = d-1 (s = beta). d agrees with promote_distance for H >= 5. For H = 4 the double step would promote, so H >= 5 is needed.\n- (H3) gives a legal king move to t even in check. t is empty, not attacked by a pawn (F1) and not adjacent to beta's king. (H2) via (F1) gives that beta is never in check.\n- Corollary 2.11a against `_child` for every terminal type:\n  - A one-sided passer gives b = the passer's owner. A race gives b = N iff dp(N) <= dp(M), which is exactly the count-rule test dp(opponent) >= e+1.\n  - The remark dp(omega) >= mu_omega holds: 0 failures in 377,608 random tests on cyl:8x6.\n  - e = d for b = M and e = d-1 for b = N both match H5.\n- Corollary 2.11b is correct. In the cyl:8x6 layout, (H2), (H3) and the king clause of (H1) hold for either colour as beta.\n\nCOMPUTATION (my own code in the scratchpad k10r2, not K10/K10v code; kpsolve --frozen --cap 1 under nice, 2 threads, one process at a time):\n- Own checker for (H1)-(H5), with its own G_omega search, against kpsolve --frozen: about 33,000 certified instances over boards cyl:4x6, 5x5, 5x6, 6x5, 6x6, 7x5, 7x6, 8x5 and 8x6, with e.p. rights, random kings, parked kings and narrow file windows. 0 violations.\n- Power check: dropping H1, H2, H4 or H5 gives violations (259, 260, 1 and 925 of about 2,500). Dropping H3 gave none in that random sample, but the note's own H3 example reproduces.\n- Corollary 2.11a, terminal by terminal: every certified terminal of all four kinds (single/M, single/N, race/M, race/N) is a b-win in kpsolve. 0 violations.\n- P11 end to end, exhaustive in the layout: boards cyl:6x6, 8x6 and 7x6 with sizes as in the Checks paragraph (56,872 lines, each a (position, side to move) pair).\n  - 48,000 conservative certified wins, 0 violations.\n  - 26,014 lines have every terminal certified; on them --frozen equals the D-result (0 differences).\n  - 3,194 lines differ from the D-result, and every one has an uncertified terminal.\n  - Slackening the H4 bound to rho(p)+2, or dropping H4, gives 8 violations on cyl:6x6 files c-e. The note's counts reproduce exactly.\n- All 28 examples in 'What the hypotheses do' reproduce their stated values, and the D-result claims for Pc2 Pc3 pd3 pd4 and Pc2 Pe2 Pe3 pd4 pe4 pe5 also match.\n\nCleanup note: k10r2 already held files that were not mine (ex.py, sweep.py, mine_h15). My cleanup `rm` of *.res, *.pos, *.json and *.err in k10r2 may have removed other agents' files of those types. The repository was not touched.",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:15:            "where": "LOOPY.md status row P11 and section 6 row P11 (scope of 'every X of the layout')",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:16:            "what": "As written, the claim '--frozen = o(W(X)) for every X of the layout whose passer and race terminals are all certified by Corollary 2.11a' is false for a root X that already has a passer. Corollary 2.11a certifies only a node y' reached by a move, so a root passer is not covered and the hypothesis holds vacuously. The Checks paragraph (K10's end-to-end run) restricts to roots with no passer, but P11 does not say so.",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:18:            "fix": "Add 'X has no passer' to P11 in both places. Alternatively require that X itself satisfy (H1)-(H5) for its passer, with s the side to move; Proposition 2.11 applies at any legal y, not only at nodes reached by a move."
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:22:            "where": "Section 6 row P11, Basis column: '(frozen kings satisfy V1 and V3 by fiat)'",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:23:            "what": "Only (V3) holds by fiat. (V1) does not follow from freezing the kings: checks and stalemate occur in --frozen when kings are not parked (the note's own (H3) example `Ke1 ka6 Pa4 Pc5 Pg5 pa5 b -` is (D,D)). In the layout, (V1) holds because no check is possible and a king move to a2/a5 is always legal (Corollary 2.11b). This contradicts the status row, which attributes (V1) to the layout.",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:25:            "fix": "Replace the parenthesis by '(V3) by fiat; (V1a, no check) and (V1b) from the layout, as in Corollary 2.11b: a2/a5 is always a legal king move'."
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:29:            "where": "Status row P11: '(V1) from the layout (no check is possible, and a king move is always legal)'",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:30:            "what": "(V1a) of Theorem 1.14 also includes condition (E). The status row does not list it, while the section 6 row does. Also (V2) of Theorem 1.14 covers the Z-move terminals, which are P-wins for the opponent by fiat. The row mentions Corollary 2.11a only for passer and race terminals.",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:36:            "where": "Section 6 row P11: 'hereditarily sum-safe (HSS) means that every component of X is itself sum-safe in the sense of Theorem 1.14(ii)'",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:43:            "where": "Proposition 2.11, Setting and Step 1 ('ω's king gives no check')",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:44:            "what": "The proof uses that y is a legal position: the kings are not adjacent. Without that, ω's king can attack β's king, and H2 (pawns only) does not exclude it. The statement says only 'fix a position y before any promotion'. All uses (Corollary 2.11a, kpsolve inputs) are legal, so this is a drafting gap, not a counterexample.",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:50:            "where": "Corollary 2.11b, '(H3) holds with t = a5 ... (the kings are four ranks apart)'",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:67:        "summary": "Result: no violation of Proposition 2.11 in about 6.8 million certified instances, and no wrong example. I found two small wording defects in the surrounding text.\n\nSetup. I wrote my own checker for (H1)-(H5) as worded in the note. It has its own move generator and legality test, and computes mu_omega by iterative-deepening search over G_omega. The brute-force reference was a private kpsolve build run with --frozen, --threads 2 and nice 10, one process at a time. Peak memory was 1.8 GB. Only my own pids were involved and no pattern kills were used. Another process shared the k10r2 directory and overwrote my first chk.py and kp. I moved to k10r2/mine_h15/, rebuilt there, and never touched K10/ or K10v/ outputs. The big scratch files are deleted; the scripts, logs and result JSONs (268 KB) remain in mine_h15/.\n\nTest design. I used no cylcgt code except where the task named it, in the dp and credit comparison. For every legal position y and side to move s, I tested every beta in {W,B} and every pawn p of beta. If (H1)-(H5) held, kpsolve had to report a beta win under both of its bounds. Positions that kpsolve rejected as illegal (result X) were recorded; there were none, so my legality test agrees with kpsolve's.\n\nInstances tested. Counts are not de-duplicated across runs. A \"position\" means a position with side to move, and an \"instance\" means (position, beta, p).\n- Cylinder sweep, all boards: 15.89M legal positions generated, 5,730,596 certified positions, 6,783,482 certified instances. Violations: 0. Positions where both colours were certified: 0.\n- Boards: cyl:5x6, 6x6, 7x6, 8x6 and 6x7 as asked, plus 6x5 and 4x6.\n- Kings parked on the a-file, exhaustive over the pawn configurations:\n  - 5x6, files c-d, up to 3 per side: 103,240 positions, 120,088 instances.\n  - 6x6, files c-e, up to 3: 1,655,328 positions, 2,008,164 instances.\n  - 7x6, files c-f, up to 2: 619,912 positions, 711,536 instances.\n  - 8x6, files c-g, up to 2: 1,662,392 positions, 1,943,352 instances.\n  - 6x7, files c-e, up to 2: 626,136 positions, 693,216 instances.\n  - Here the kings stand on all 20 or 21 legal a-file pairs.\n- Kings free and exhaustive: 5x6 with pawns on c-d, up to 3 per side, all legal king pairs: 334,788 positions, 383,076 instances. 4x6: 19,072 positions.\n- Random scenes built for the tight cases, 600k per board: 273,497 certified positions in total (13,354 on 5x6; 35,557 on 6x6; 63,440 on 7x6; 91,893 on 8x6; 35,933 on 6x7; 33,320 on 6x5).\n- Uniform random pawns, kings free: 265,911 certified positions on the five required boards. Same with kings parked: 170,320.\n- Flat boards (the note says \"not run\"): 434,429 certified positions, 516,823 instances, 0 violations.\n- Coverage of the tight cases among the certified cylinder instances, counted by a feature tagger:\n  - H5 tight (mu = e+1): 3,306,911.\n  - Another beta pawn at rho(p)+1 on an adjacent file (H4 at its bound): 242,044.\n  - A beta pawn behind p on p's file with a capture available: 596,788.\n  - An omega pawn entering the strip by a capture from two files away: 307,127.\n  - E.p. right for omega against another beta pawn: 333,474, of which 99,665 are capturable at once and 44,021 have the victim on an adjacent file.\n  - p gives check to omega's king: 26,414.\n  - Omega in check at y: 48,190.\n  - Omega has a passer, so a race: 4,642,279.\n  - Black as beta: 3,392,112 (White as beta: 3,391,370).\n  - Kings not both on the a-file: 845,614.\n\nHarness sensitivity. When a hypothesis is relaxed, kpsolve does show non-wins, so the zero-violation result is not vacuous.\n- Parked exhaustive 6x6:\n  - Dropping H4: 24 of 940 fail. The same 24 fail with the bound relaxed to rho(p)+2, so the bound is tight; the example is `Ka1 ka6 Pe2 pc5 pd3 pe5 w -`.\n  - Weakening H5 to mu >= e: 6,891 of 7,855 fail.\n  - Dropping the e.p. clause of H1: 272 of 692 fail.\n- Random kings, depending on board:\n  - Dropping H2: 308 of 4,826 on 5x6 and 555 of 5,059 on 7x6.\n  - Dropping the king clause of H1: 124 of 499 on 5x6 and 711 of 1,767 on 8x6.\n  - Dropping H3: 1 to 12 failures per board.\n\nOther claims checked.\n- Corollary 2.11b: on cyl:8x6 with Ka1 ka6 and pawns on c-g (up to 2 per side, 371,250 instances), H2, H3 and the king clause of H1 held every time.\n- Count-rule remark: my dp agrees with cylcgt promote_distance on 438,320 comparisons, and dp(omega) >= mu_omega held in all 378,407 instances where no king stands on the fastest passer's path. Whenever mu_omega exceeded dp, a king stood on that path.\n- Reproduction of the \"parked\" counts, for the positions the note calls \"instances\": 74,410 on 6x6 up to 3, 64,954 on 8x6 up to 2, 306,020 on 6x7 up to 3, 12,160 on 6x5 up to 3 and 496,848 on 7x6 up to 3, all exactly. K10v's 91,074 instances on 6x6 and 29,140 on 7x6 up to 2 are also exact.\n\nExamples. I re-ran all 27 positions of the list (25 on cyl:8x6, one on cyl:7x6, one on flat:7x6) with kpsolve --frozen --cap 1, and all stated results match. Every stated hypothesis status and value of d, e and mu also matches. I also confirmed with cylcgt that `W: c2 c3 / B: d3 d4` is a White win in the D-model, and that `W: c2 e2 e3 / B: d4 e4 e5` is too. In `Pc2 Pd4 pd3` only White has a passer, and in the race `Pc4 Pd2 pe3 pf4` dp(W)=2 and dp(B)=3.\n\nProof. I read the proof (Steps 0-5) against the code. Step 2 holds, in particular the bound rho <= rho_p+1 for captures landing in the strip, the e.p. case, and the double step. Step 4 (G_omega only enlarges omega's move set) holds, and Step 5 is correct with e = d or d-1. Step 1 holds because t is never attacked or occupied. I found no gap in the proof, and no counterexample from any run.",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:71:            "where": "Corollary 2.11b, first sentence",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:73:            "counterexample": "cyl:8x6 `Ka1 ka6 Pa3 Pc2 w -`: the position is legal, the kings stand on a1 and a6, and the b- and h-files are empty. For beta = White with p = c2, my checker gives H1, H2, H4 and H5 true and H3 false. Omega's king on a6 has no neighbouring square whose three files are free of pawns. This is a wording defect only: the P11 layout row says 'pawn components on c-g', and for pawns on c-g the corollary holds in all 371,250 instances tested.",
kings/delegate/out/K10r2/verdicts.json:78:            "where": "Checks paragraph (K10 fixer), and the matching figure in status row R2.11",
